10:32:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Horses do **not** build large muscles from straw alone. They still need protein, energy, vitamins, and minerals—just from different foods and via a different digestive system. - **Horses normally eat forage, not just straw.** Grass, hay, legumes such as alfalfa, and sometimes grains provide protein. Typical grass hay may contain roughly 8–15% protein; alfalfa can contain more. Straw is mostly low-nutrient fiber and is generally used as bedding or a minor roughage source, not as the whole diet. - **They are adapted to extract energy from fiber.** Horses have a large cecum and colon full of microbes that ferment plant fiber into fatty acids, which supply a lot of their energy. Humans cannot digest cellulose this way to anything like the same extent. - **But horses still require amino acids.** Muscle is made from protein, and protein is made of amino acids. Horses obtain these largely from plant proteins in grass, hay, leaves, seeds, and feed. A horse fed only poor straw would lose condition and muscle, grow poorly, and become unhealthy. - **Their “massive” musculature is also genetic and anatomical.** Horses are large animals shaped by evolution and, in many breeds, selective breeding for running, pulling, or carrying. Their muscles are not evidence that protein is unnecessary; they are evidence that their diet and physiology meet their protein needs. Humans also do not need animal protein specifically—we can get enough protein from beans, lentils, soy, grains, nuts, seeds, and other plant foods. The key difference is that humans cannot turn fibrous grass or straw into useful calories and protein nearly as effectively as a horse can.
