10:33:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your uncle may simply be using “straw” as the everyday word for dried forage. That is common in casual speech, especially if the distinction does not matter for the conversation. But in animal nutrition, **hay and straw are different materials**: - **Hay** = dried grass, clover, alfalfa, etc., harvested as feed. It has meaningful protein and calories. - **Straw** = mostly stems left after grain crops like wheat, oats, or barley are harvested. It is much lower in nutrients and often used as bedding. So he may genuinely feed horses what he calls straw, while it is technically hay—or he may feed a mixture, including some actual straw. Either way, successful horse raising for 30 years strongly implies the horses are receiving adequate nutrition somewhere in the system: good forage, pasture, grain/feed, mineral supplementation, or all of these. The core point is not that your uncle is wrong about what he calls it. It is that a horse cannot maintain substantial muscle indefinitely on actual cereal straw alone. That is basic biology: muscle requires amino acids, and straw generally does not supply enough usable protein or total nutrition by itself.
