10:33:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Elephants and horses can become very large on plant-based diets because plants contain protein and because both animals are highly adapted to eating large amounts of fibrous vegetation. But that still does not mean “any dry plant material,” such as straw, is nutritionally enough by itself. A few key distinctions: - **Leaves and grass are not equivalent to straw.** Fresh grass, browse, leaves, twigs, and many hays contain more protein, minerals, and digestible energy than cereal straw. Straw is largely the tough, low-nutrient stem remaining after the grain has been harvested. - **Elephants eat huge quantities and a varied diet.** Wild elephants may consume roughly 100–200+ kg of plant material per day, depending on size and conditions. They eat grasses, leaves, bark, roots, fruit, branches, and other browse. In managed settings, diets are often supplemented with hay, produce, formulated feeds, minerals, etc. - **Their gut microbes help, but do not create a complete diet from nothing.** Both elephants and horses use microbes in their hindgut to ferment fiber into energy. Microbes can contribute some protein and vitamins, but the animal still needs sufficient dietary nitrogen/protein, minerals, and calories. - **Size does not require a meat-based or high-protein diet.** Large herbivores grow slowly over years and eat for much of the day. A modest percentage of protein multiplied by a very large total food intake can provide plenty of amino acids. Adult muscle maintenance also requires less protein than rapid growth or intense training. A horse could maintain muscle on a well-designed forage diet—good pasture or quality hay, often with salt/minerals and sometimes additional feed. But if “straw” means genuine wheat/barley/oat straw as the overwhelming or sole food source, it would generally be too low in usable nutrients. It is closer to eating dry cardboard-like plant stalks than to eating a balanced mix of grass and leaves.
